 "location": {
  "name": "Mont-Saint-Michel",
  "wiki": "Mont-Saint-Michel",
  "country": "France",
  "region": "Manche, Normandy",
  "lat": 48.636,
  "lon": -1.511,
  "tagline": "A medieval abbey crowning a tidal island that the sea surrounds twice a day.",
  "intro": "Mont-Saint-Michel is a rocky tidal island about a kilometre off the Normandy coast, topped by a Benedictine abbey whose spire rises to 92 m above sea level. Legend says the archangel Michael told Bishop Aubert of Avranches to build a church here in 708. Today it draws more than three million visitors a year, making it France's most-visited attraction outside Paris.",
  "stats": [
   {
    "label": "Highest point",
    "value": "92 m (302 ft)"
   },
   {
    "label": "Tidal range",
    "value": "≈14 m (46 ft)"
   },
   {
    "label": "Residents",
    "value": "23 (2023)"
   },
   {
    "label": "UNESCO World Heritage",
    "value": "Since 1979"
   }
  ],
  "facts": [
   "During the Hundred Years' War the English besieged it twice (1423 and 1433) and failed both times; in 1434 they left behind two wrought-iron bombards.",
   "After the French Revolution the abbey became a prison until 1863, and in 1944 it briefly held collaborators after the liberation.",
   "The light footbridge to the island, opened in 2014 for €209 million, lets the tide flow freely around it; a 'supertide' on 21 March 2015 submerged it completely.",
   "Medieval pilgrims called it 'St. Michael in peril of the sea' because the incoming tide could trap those crossing the sands.",
   "Artist Alan Lee drew on the mount when designing Minas Tirith for The Lord of the Rings."
